.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt STRFMON 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm strfmon
.Nd convert monetary value to string
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In monetary.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn strfmon "char * restrict s" "size_t maxsize" "const char * restrict format"  "double value" "..."
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn strfmon_l "char * restrict s" "size_t maxsize" "locale_t loc" "const char * restrict format" "double value" "..."
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn strfmon
function places characters into the array pointed to by
.Fa s
as controlled by the string pointed to by
.Fa format .
No more than
.Fa maxsize
bytes are placed into the array.
.Pp
The
.Fn strfmon_l
function behaves the same as
.Fn strfmon ,
but uses the locale
.Fa loc
instead of the current locale of the process.
.Pp
The format string is composed of zero or more directives:
ordinary characters (anything other than
.Sq Cm %
which introduces conversions, and
.Sq Cm \e0
which ends the format string),
which are copied unchanged to the output stream;
and conversion specifications,
each of which may result in fetching a subsequent
.Em value
argument.
Each conversion specification is introduced by the
.Cm %
character.
After the
.Cm % ,
any of the following may appear, but only in the order shown here:
.Bl -bullet -width 3m
.It
Zero or more of the following flags,
no more than once each,
in any order:
.Bl -tag -width "XXX"
.It Cm = Ns Ar f
An
.Sq Cm =
character followed by another character
.Ar f
which is used as the numeric fill character.
This character must be one which occupies exactly 1 column of output
when using a fixed width font (mono spaced) to display it.
If not the results are unspecified.
This implementation ignores attempts to specify fill characters
that are not 1 column wide.
Others might treat this as a format error,
or simply attempt to use the fill character specified,
meaning that the alignment expected would not be obtained.
The default is a space
.Pq Sq \ \& .
This character is used only when accompanied by a value
for the
.Cm "left precision"
as specified below.
.It Cm ^
Do not use grouping characters in the integral part of the value,
regardless of the current locale default.
When this flag is not used, the integral part of the value might
have grouping characters
.Pq thousands separators ,
as specified by the locale, included
in the value at positions specified by the locale
.Pq often before each group of three digits which is preceded by more digits .
No grouping characters are inserted amongst the fill characters,
even if the fill character happens to be a digit,
and nor amongst any digits that follow the radix character (decimal point).
.It Cm +
Represent positive values in the locale's default formats,
using the locale's sign strings.
This requires the locale to be defined in such a way that the
difference between positive and negative values can be determined.
That can be by different representations of the positive and negative
sign strings, or by different positioning.
It is an error if
.Cm +
is specified, and the sign of the value would be unable to be
determined from the result.
If the locale specifies that parentheses are normally used,
rather than the appropriate sign string,
the sign string will be placed before the value, and also
before the currency symbol, if that is included in the output
preceding the value.
The C
.Pq aka POSIX
locale is one in which the
.Cm +
flag will fail, as it has no sign strings defined.
.Pp
The default is the locale's preferred representation,
often similar to this format,
while allowing much locale data to be substituted by
reasonable defaults if required,
such as using the
.Dq \&\-
character to indicate negative values if no sign strings exist,
or placing a positive sign after the value and a negative
sign before it,
if the sign strings are identical.
.It Cm \&(
Enclose negative values in parentheses,
and positive values (or zero) with no sign indication.
When this flag is used,
the locale's sign strings and their positioning
are irrelevant.
Only one of the
.Cm \&+
and
.Cm \&(
flags may be included.
Neither is required.
.It Cm \&!
Do not include a currency symbol in the output.
The default is to include either the national currency symbol,
or its international equivalent three letter form,
if defined by the locale,
at a position in the output defined by the locale.
.It Cm \-
Left justify the result.
Only meaningful when a field width is specified.
The default is for the result to be right justified within
the specified field width.
.El
.It
A minimum field width as an unsigned decimal number.
By default, there is no minimum width.
This specifies the number of columns
.Pq each wide enough for a single space character of a fixed width font
the output is to occupy,
and is unrelated to the number of bytes required to represent that string.
The resulting formatted value is right justified within a
field of this width (left justified if the
.Cm \-
flag was given) with unoccupied positions filled with spaces.
.It
A
.Sq Cm #
sign followed by an unsigned decimal number specifying the
maximum expected number of digits before the radix character.
This is the
.Dq Cm "Left Precision"
of the result.
When this option is used, values that do not exceed the
specified number of digits before the radix are formatted so
they will be correctly aligned with other values generated
using the same format and locale,
provided the results are displayed in columns using a fixed width font.
Space is allowed for the number of digits specified, plus, 
if grouping could be performed and has not been suppressed,
the maximum possible number of thousands
grouping characters that would be required by a value with
the specified maximum number of digits.
The fill character 
.Po
see the
.Sq Cm \&=
flag above
.Pc
is used to fill positions not used by a particular value.
In no case is the value truncated by the left precision specified.
Larger values will be represented correctly,
but will not align with values that have no more than the
specified left precision number of pre-radix digits.
In addition, use of the left precision causes spaces to be inserted
at any location where a value of the opposite sign,
would insert a sign string or currency symbol, where the
actual
.Ar value
inserts nothing, or a string of lesser width.
The locale may also specify extra spaces in the output.
All the generated converted 
.Ar value Ns s ,
for any
.Ar value
which needs no more
that the specified number of pre-radix digits,
will occupy the same number of columns of output.
.It
A
.Sq Cm \&.
character followed by an unsigned decimal number specifying
the number of digits to be placed after the radix character,
known as the
.Dq Cm "Right Precision" .
The default is supplied by the locale
.Pq often two .
If the locale defines no radix character
.Pq decimal point ,
for either monetary or normal numeric values,
then this value is always treated as if it were zero.
The radix character, and following digits,
are included in the output if, and only if,
the effective value of this parameter is not zero.
.El
.Pp
The conversion specification is terminated with one
of the following conversion type indicator characters:
.Bl -tag -width 3m
.It Cm i
The next argument is treated as a
.Vt double
to be formatted as an international monetary amount.
.It Cm n
The next argument is treated as a
.Vt double
to be formatted as a national monetary amount.
.It Cm %
A
.Sq Li %
character is written.
No argument is consumed.
No flags, widths, or precisions are permitted, the
whole conversion must be
.Dq %% .
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
If all specified conversions succeed,
and the total number of resulting bytes including the terminating
.Dv NUL
byte is not more than
.Fa maxsize ,
.Fn strfmon
returns the number of bytes placed into the array pointed to by
.Fa s ,
not including the terminating
.Dv NUL
byte.
Otherwise, \-1 is returned,
the contents of the array are indeterminate,
and
.Va errno
is set to indicate the error.
.Sh ERRORS
The
.Fn strfmon
function will fail if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er E2BIG
Conversion stopped due to lack of space in the array
.Ar s .
.It Bq Er EILSEQ
An invalid character encoding was detected in the format string.
In some instances, badly formatted characters in the locale data
might also generate this error.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The format string is invalid.
This includes if it requests output which
.Pq given the specification of the locale in question
would not not allow all possible values to be correctly interpreted.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
Not enough memory for temporary buffers.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr localeconv 3 ,
.Xr nls 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Fn strfmon
function
conforms to
.St -p1003.1-2001
as updated by
.St -p1003.1-2024
and the resolution of Austin Group issue 2000, expected to be
incorporated in a Technical Corrigendum to the latter.
